Time Invariance of Dynamical Systems
A system is said to be time-invariant iff for any admissible pair
{x(t0), u[t0,∞)} → {x[t0,∞), y[t0,∞)}
and any real number α, the pair
{Qαx(t0), Qαu[t0,∞)} → {Qαx[t0,∞), Qαy[t0,∞)}
is also admissible. Otherwise, the system is said to be time-varying.
• For same initial states and waveform of excitations, waveform of responses will always be the same, no matter what instant the excitations are applied.
• For LTI systems, x ̇ = Ax + Bu(t), y = Cx + Eu(t)
• For LTI systems, responses are independent of initial time, thus t0 = 0.
• State-space: n-dimensional real vector space (Rn, R)
• A: Linear operator that maps ∑ to itself, with basis vectors ni
• Dynamical systems are all causal.
